@bc_help = dso_local constant [6507 x i8] c"usage: %s [options] [file...]\0A\0Abc is a command-line, arbitrary-precision calculator with a Turing-complete\0Alanguage. For details, use `man %s` or see the online documentation at\0Ahttps://github.com/gavinhoward/bc/tree/%s/manuals/bc/%s.1.md .\0A\0AThis bc is compatible with both the GNU bc and the POSIX bc spec. See the GNU bc\0Amanual (https://www.gnu.org/software/bc/manual/bc.html) and bc spec\0A(http://pubs.opengroup.org/onlinepubs/9699919799/utilities/bc.html)\0Afor details.\0A\0AThis bc has three differences to the GNU bc:\0A\0A  1) Arrays can be passed to the builtin \22length\22 function to get the number of\0A     elements currently in the array. The following example prints \221\22:\0A\0A       a[0] = 0\0A       length(a[])\0A\0A  2) The precedence of the boolean \22not\22 operator (!) is equal to that of the\0A     unary minus (-), or negation, operator. This still allows POSIX-compliant\0A     scripts to work while somewhat preserving expected behavior (versus C) and\0A     making parsing easier.\0A  3) This bc has many more extensions than the GNU bc does. For details, see the\0A     man page or online documentation.\0A\0AThis bc also implements the dot (.) extension of the BSD bc.\0A\0AOptions:\0A\0A  -C  --no-digit-clamp\0A\0A      Disables clamping of digits that are larger than or equal to the current\0A      ibase when parsing numbers.\0A\0A      This means that the value added to a number from a digit is always that\0A      digit's value multiplied by the value of ibase raised to the power of the\0A      digit's position, which starts from 0 at the least significant digit.\0A\0A      If multiple of this option and the -c option are given, the last is used.\0A\0A  -c  --digit-clamp\0A\0A      Enables clamping of digits that are larger than or equal to the current\0A      ibase when parsing numbers.\0A\0A      This means that digits that the value added to a number from a digit that\0A      is greater than or equal to the ibase is the value of ibase minus 1 all\0A      multiplied by the value of ibase raised to the power of the digit's\0A      position, which starts from 0 at the least significant digit.\0A\0A      If multiple of this option and the -C option are given, the last is used.\0A\0A  -E  seed  --seed=seed\0A\0A      Sets the builtin variable seed to the given value assuming that the given\0A      value is in base 10. It is a fatal error if the given value is not a valid\0A      number.\0A\0A  -e  expr  --expression=expr\0A\0A      Run \22expr\22 and quit. If multiple expressions or files (see below) are\0A      given, they are all run before executing from stdin.\0A\0A  -f  file  --file=file\0A\0A      Run the bc code in \22file\22 and exit. See above as well.\0A\0A  -g  --global-stacks\0A\0A      Turn scale, ibase, and obase into stacks. This makes the value of each be\0A      be restored on returning from functions. See the man page or online\0A      documentation for more details.\0A\0A  -h  --help\0A\0A      Print this usage message and exit.\0A\0A  -I  ibase  --ibase=ibase\0A\0A      Sets the builtin variable ibase to the given value assuming that the given\0A      value is in base 10. It is a fatal error if the given value is not a valid\0A      number.\0A\0A  -i  --interactive\0A\0A      Force interactive mode.\0A\0A  -L  --no-line-length\0A\0A      Disable line length checking.\0A\0A  -l  --mathlib\0A\0A      Use predefined math routines:\0A\0A          s(expr)  =  sine of expr in radians\0A          c(expr)  =  cosine of expr in radians\0A          a(expr)  =  arctangent of expr, returning radians\0A          l(expr)  =  natural log of expr\0A          e(expr)  =  raises e to the power of expr\0A          j(n, x)  =  Bessel function of integer order n of x\0A\0A      This bc may load more functions with these options. See the manpage or\0A      online documentation for details.\0A\0A  -O  obase  --obase=obase\0A\0A      Sets the builtin variable obase to the given value assuming that the given\0A      value is in base 10. It is a fatal error if the given value is not a valid\0A      number.\0A\0A  -P  --no-prompt\0A\0A      Disable the prompts in interactive mode.\0A\0A  -R  --no-read-prompt\0A\0A      Disable the read prompt in interactive mode.\0A\0A  -r  keyword  --redefine=keyword\0A\0A      Redefines \22keyword\22 and allows it to be used as a function, variable, and\0A      array name. This is useful when this bc gives parse errors on scripts\0A      meant for other bc implementations.\0A\0A      Only keywords that are not in the POSIX bc spec may be redefined.\0A\0A      It is a fatal error to attempt to redefine a keyword that cannot be\0A      redefined or does not exist.\0A\0A  -q  --quiet\0A\0A      Don't print version and copyright.\0A\0A  -S  scale  --scale=scale\0A\0A      Sets the builtin variable scale to the given value assuming that the given\0A      value is in base 10. It is a fatal error if the given value is not a valid\0A      number.\0A\0A  -s  --standard\0A\0A      Error if any non-POSIX extensions are used.\0A\0A  -w  --warn\0A\0A      Warn if any non-POSIX extensions are used.\0A\0A  -v  --version\0A\0A      Print version information and copyright and exit.\0A\0A  -z  --leading-zeroes\0A\0A      Enable leading zeroes on numbers greater than -1 and less than 1.\0A\0AEnvironment variables:\0A\0A  POSIXLY_CORRECT\0A\0A      Error if any non-POSIX extensions are used.\0A\0A  BC_ENV_ARGS\0A\0A      Command-line arguments to use on every run.\0A\0A  BC_LINE_LENGTH\0A\0A      If an integer, the number of characters to print on a line before\0A      wrapping. Using 0 will disable line length checking.\0A\0A  BC_BANNER\0A\0A      If an integer and non-zero, display the copyright banner in interactive\0A      mode.\0A\0A      If zero, disable the banner.\0A\0A      Overrides the default, which is %s print the banner.\0A\0A  BC_SIGINT_RESET\0A\0A      If an integer and non-zero, reset on SIGINT, rather than exit, when in\0A      interactive mode.\0A\0A      If zero, do not reset on SIGINT in all cases, but exit instead.\0A\0A      Overrides the default, which is %s.\0A\0A  BC_TTY_MODE\0A\0A      If an integer and non-zero, enable TTY mode when it is available.\0A\0A      If zero, disable TTY mode in all cases.\0A\0A      Overrides the default, which is TTY mode %s.\0A\0A  BC_PROMPT\0A\0A      If an integer and non-zero, enable prompt when TTY mode is possible.\0A\0A      If zero, disable prompt in all cases.\0A\0A      Overrides the default, which is prompt %s.\0A\0A  BC_EXPR_EXIT\0A\0A      If an integer and non-zero, exit when expressions or expression files are\0A      given on the command-line, and does not exit when an integer and zero.\0A\0A      Overrides the default, which is %s.\0A\0A  BC_DIGIT_CLAMP\0A\0A      If an integer and non-zero, clamp digits larger than or equal to the\0A      current ibase when parsing numbers.\0A\0A      Overrides the default, which is %s.\0A\00", align 16
